1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:02,923
Meet slack-tracker.

2
00:00:03,223 --> 00:00:07,090
Simple Slack logging for Node.js
and browser applications,

3
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:09,271
using Slack Incoming Webhooks.

4
00:00:10,821 --> 00:00:15,914
It sends formatted logs, block messages,
and raw Slack payloads, to a Slack channel.

5
00:00:16,164 --> 00:00:18,933
Server-side code sends directly to Slack.

6
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:22,425
Browser code sends to your
own backend proxy route,

7
00:00:22,445 --> 00:00:24,803
so your webhook URL stays private.

8
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:27,766
Here's what's inside.

9
00:00:27,916 --> 00:00:33,818
Node.js and browser support, TypeScript
declarations, and Incoming Webhook support.

10
00:00:33,968 --> 00:00:37,820
Direct server-side delivery,
and browser-safe proxy delivery.

11
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:41,844
Simple log levels with Slack
colors, and raw Block Kit payloads.

12
00:00:41,994 --> 00:00:46,036
Plus a build-before-publish workflow,
that ships only the dist folder.

13
00:00:47,686 --> 00:00:49,823
Installation is a single command.

14
00:00:50,023 --> 00:00:53,913
Use npm, yarn, or pnpm,
whichever you prefer.

15
00:00:55,762 --> 00:00:57,492
Want to see it in action first?

16
00:00:57,642 --> 00:00:59,815
Open the live demo on StackBlitz.

17
00:01:00,015 --> 00:01:06,451
Or download the code, run npm start in
the demo folder, and open localhost:3030.

18
00:01:08,301 --> 00:01:09,840
Next, configuration.

19
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:14,195
Call slackLogConfig() once, when
your app starts. In your bootloader,

20
00:01:14,215 --> 00:01:16,815
app constructor, or server startup code.

21
00:01:17,015 --> 00:01:22,464
The webhook URL is required on the
server. Enable turns logging on or off.

22
00:01:22,484 --> 00:01:25,812
And proxy URL is an
optional browser route.

23
00:01:26,012 --> 00:01:29,745
If it isn't called before logging,
the package prints a console error,

24
00:01:29,765 --> 00:01:30,880
and skips the log.

25
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:33,842
Now, start logging.

26
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:38,438
Call slack.log, with a label, any
data, and an optional log level.

27
00:01:38,688 --> 00:01:39,784
Server started.

28
00:01:39,884 --> 00:01:41,014
User created.

29
00:01:41,114 --> 00:01:42,541
A validation warning.

30
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:44,081
And an unhandled error.

31
00:01:44,332 --> 00:01:47,102
Each one lands in your
channel, neatly formatted.

32
00:01:48,952 --> 00:01:50,579
There are five log levels.

33
00:01:50,729 --> 00:01:51,463
Default.

34
00:01:51,483 --> 00:01:52,297
Success.

35
00:01:52,317 --> 00:01:53,039
Info.

36
00:01:53,059 --> 00:01:53,706
Warn.

37
00:01:53,726 --> 00:01:54,512
And error.

38
00:01:54,762 --> 00:01:59,011
Each level adds a label, an icon,
and a color, to the Slack message.

39
00:02:00,662 --> 00:02:02,890
Need several values in one message?

40
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,778
Use logBlockMessage, to send
multiple titled values.

41
00:02:06,978 --> 00:02:11,773
An order ID, an amount, and a customer
object, all in one tidy block.

42
00:02:13,622 --> 00:02:16,301
And when you need full
control, use slack.raw.

43
00:02:16,501 --> 00:02:21,067
Send any Slack payload you like, with
Block Kit sections, context, and more.

44
00:02:23,117 --> 00:02:25,378
You can even send interactive buttons.

45
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:30,019
Add an actions block with two buttons,
and slack.raw sends it, as is.

46
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:33,971
To make clicks do something,
enable Slack app interactivity,

47
00:02:33,991 --> 00:02:37,676
and handle the block_actions
payload, for each action_id.

48
00:02:39,725 --> 00:02:41,809
Browser usage is supported too.

49
00:02:41,959 --> 00:02:46,399
In the browser, logs are never
sent directly to Slack. Instead,

50
00:02:46,419 --> 00:02:50,675
they're posted to /api/slack-tracker,
on your own backend.

51
00:02:50,875 --> 00:02:53,907
So keep your webhook URL
out of browser config.

52
00:02:54,107 --> 00:02:57,548
And set a proxy URL, only
when your route is different.

53
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:03,107
On the server, handleSlackLogsRequest
does the heavy lifting.

54
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:05,622
Drop it into a Next.js route handler.

55
00:03:05,772 --> 00:03:07,009
Or an Express route.

56
00:03:07,209 --> 00:03:12,155
Your webhook URL comes from an environment
variable, and never leaves the server.

57
00:03:14,005 --> 00:03:16,166
Here's the full API, at a glance.

58
00:03:16,316 --> 00:03:19,060
slack.log, for formatted messages.

59
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:21,519
logBlockMessage, for titled fields.

60
00:03:21,569 --> 00:03:23,286
slack.raw, for custom payloads.

61
00:03:23,336 --> 00:03:27,040
handleSlackLogsRequest,
for browser proxy requests.

62
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:31,556
And slackLogConfig(), which stores your
config once, before any of them run.

63
00:03:33,406 --> 00:03:34,675
That's slack-tracker.

64
00:03:34,875 --> 00:03:37,171
Configure it once, and log from anywhere.

